---
import ChapterLayout from '../layouts/ChapterLayout.astro';
import Section from '../components/content/Section.astro';
import Cite from '../components/content/Cite.astro';
import Note from '../components/content/Note.astro';
import Placeholder from '../components/content/Placeholder.astro';
import TechDetail from '../components/content/TechDetail.astro';
import Table from '../components/content/Table.astro';
import SbolConstruct from '../components/science/SbolConstruct.astro';
import ModuleExplorer from '../components/science/ModuleExplorer.astro';
import { href } from '../lib/paths';

const toc = [
  { id: 'anatomy', title: 'Anatomy of a sensing module' },
  { id: 'the-array', title: 'Six modules, three families' },
  { id: 'design-choices', title: 'Design choices we are weighing' },
  { id: 'modularity', title: 'Why modularity matters here' },
  { id: 'parts', title: 'Parts' },
];

const refs = ['radhika2007', 'hakim2012'];
---

<ChapterLayout
  slug="synthetic-biology"
  toc={toc}
  refs={refs}
  brief={[
    'Every sensing module has the same three jobs: sense a compound, process the signal, report it in a way we can measure.',
    'Modules share an interface, so a sensing element can be swapped without redesigning the rest of the array.',
    'The specific parts are not fixed yet. Undecided parts are drawn with dashed outlines throughout this chapter.',
  ]}
>
  <Section
    id="anatomy"
    title="Anatomy of a sensing module"
    lead="A module is a small genetic circuit inside a host cell. It reads chemistry and writes light."
  >
    <p>
      The sensing part of the circuit is expressed steadily, so the cell is always ready. When the target compound is
      present, it changes the activity of a responsive promoter, which drives production of a reporter protein. The
      amount of reporter is what the instrument measures.
    </p>

    <SbolConstruct
      label="Sensing device: constitutive promoter, ribosome binding site, sensor coding sequence, terminator"
      parts={[
        { type: 'promoter', label: 'Constitutive promoter' },
        { type: 'rbs', label: 'RBS' },
        { type: 'cds', label: 'Sensor protein (to be chosen)', placeholder: true },
        { type: 'terminator', label: 'Terminator' },
      ]}
    >
      Sensing device. The sensor protein is produced continuously so that the cell can respond at any time.
    </SbolConstruct>

    <SbolConstruct
      label="Reporting device: compound-responsive promoter, ribosome binding site, reporter coding sequence, terminator"
      parts={[
        { type: 'promoter', label: 'Responsive promoter (to be chosen)', placeholder: true },
        { type: 'rbs', label: 'RBS' },
        { type: 'cds', label: 'Reporter (to be chosen)', placeholder: true },
        { type: 'terminator', label: 'Terminator' },
      ]}
    >
      Reporting device. The responsive promoter is the point where chemistry becomes gene expression, and the reporter
      is what the reader sees.
    </SbolConstruct>

    <Note kind="context" title="Sense, process, report">
      <p>
        Splitting a module into these three jobs is what makes the array extensible. Change the sensor and the
        responsive promoter, keep the reporter and the host, and the rest of the system, including the analysis, does
        not have to change.
      </p>
    </Note>
  </Section>

  <Section id="the-array" title="Six modules, three families">
    <p>
      The array is designed around the three signal families described in{' '}
      <a href={href('solution#three-families')}>Solution</a>: tumour metabolism, oxidative stress and chronic
      inflammation.<Cite id="hakim2012" /> Each module is aimed mostly at one family, with deliberate cross-reactivity to
      the others, because a set of overlapping responses carries more information than a set of narrow ones.
    </p>

    <ModuleExplorer />

    <Placeholder title="Real module definitions" where="src/data/signals.ts">
      <ul>
        <li>Target compound or compound class for each module.</li>
        <li>Sensing element: transcription factor, receptor or enzyme, with its source organism.</li>
        <li>Responsive promoter and reporter, with registry part numbers where they exist.</li>
        <li>Host strain, and why it was chosen.</li>
      </ul>
    </Placeholder>
  </Section>

  <Section id="design-choices" title="Design choices we are weighing">
    <p>
      These are the decisions in front of us. Each one is written as options with trade-offs rather than as a settled
      answer, because they are not settled.
    </p>

    <TechDetail summary="Sensing element: transcription-factor biosensor, receptor, or enzyme-coupled">
      <p>
        <strong>Transcription-factor biosensors</strong> use a regulator that binds a small molecule and changes
        transcription at its promoter. They are well established in bacteria and give a direct route from compound to
        gene expression, but each one is specific to the compounds its regulator evolved to bind.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Olfactory receptors</strong> detect volatile compounds in animals. A yeast strain carrying the main
        components of the mammalian olfactory signalling pathway, coupled to a fluorescent reporter, has been used to
        screen receptors against a volatile target.<Cite id="radhika2007" /> That is a precedent for volatile sensing in
        engineered cells, though such systems are harder to build and tune than a transcription-factor circuit.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Enzyme-coupled sensing</strong> converts a compound into something a cell already senses. It adds a step
        but can extend the range of detectable compounds.
      </p>
    </TechDetail>

    <TechDetail summary="Reporter: fluorescent protein, luciferase, or colour">
      <p>
        A <strong>fluorescent protein</strong> is easy to read with simple optics and gives a continuous signal, but
        needs an excitation source and has a maturation delay. <strong>Luciferase</strong> gives a strong signal with a
        very low background and no excitation light, at the cost of adding a substrate. A{' '}
        <strong>colorimetric output</strong> can be read by eye, which matters for low-resource settings, but is harder
        to quantify precisely.
      </p>
    </TechDetail>

    <TechDetail summary="Host: bacteria, yeast, or a cell-free system">
      <p>
        <strong><i>Escherichia coli</i></strong> grows fast and has the largest library of characterised parts.
        <strong><i>Saccharomyces cerevisiae</i></strong> supports eukaryotic membrane proteins such as olfactory
        receptors, which matters if we go that route.<Cite id="radhika2007" /> A <strong>cell-free system</strong>
        contains no living organism at all, which simplifies biosafety and shelf life, but reagents cost more and the
        response is short-lived.
      </p>
      <p>
        The host choice is also a safety choice, and is discussed in <a href={href('safety-and-security')}>Safety and ethics</a>.
      </p>
    </TechDetail>
  </Section>

  <Section id="modularity" title="Why modularity matters here">
    <p>
      An array is only as extensible as its interface. We are designing so that every module shares the same host, the
      same reporter and the same read-out geometry, and differs only in its sensing element and responsive promoter.
      Two consequences follow: a new module can join the array without changing the reader or the analysis, and a module
      that fails characterisation can be removed without invalidating the rest.
    </p>

    <Table label="What is shared and what varies between modules">
      <table class="data">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Layer</th>
            <th scope="col">Shared across the array</th>
            <th scope="col">Different per module</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Host</th>
            <td>Same strain and growth conditions</td>
            <td>—</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Sensing</th>
            <td>—</td>
            <td>Sensor protein and responsive promoter</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Reporting</th>
            <td>Same reporter and measurement settings</td>
            <td>—</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Analysis</th>
            <td>Same normalisation and pattern comparison</td>
            <td>Module position in the response vector</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </Table>
  </Section>

  <Section id="parts" title="Parts">
    <p>
      Parts used and contributed will be listed here with their registry numbers, so that another team can rebuild what
      we build.
    </p>
    <Placeholder title="Parts table" kind="data" where="src/pages/synthetic-biology.astro">
      <ul>
        <li>Registry part number, name and type for every part used.</li>
        <li>Which parts were newly designed by the team, and characterisation data for each.</li>
        <li>Sequences and sources, and any changes made to existing parts.</li>
      </ul>
    </Placeholder>
  </Section>
</ChapterLayout>
